{"name": "dnd", "triage": true, "claims": [{"index": 725, "feature": "plainness and economy", "claim": "Bryce Lynch says one well written specific sentence is worth more than a page of details.", "source": "Bryce Lynch, review of 'The Swan Hollow Intrigue', tenfootpole.org, 2026", "url": "https://tenfootpole.org/ironspike/?p=10575", "quote": "one well written specific sentence is worth more"}, {"index": 726, "feature": "plainness and economy", "claim": "Bryce Lynch defines specificity as terse, so that it does not interrupt the DM scanning the page.", "source": "Bryce Lynch, review of 'The Swan Hollow Intrigue', tenfootpole.org, 2026", "url": "https://tenfootpole.org/ironspike/?p=10575", "quote": "It is terse, meaning it doesn’t get in the way"}, {"index": 727, "feature": "dm-facing sentence", "claim": "Bryce Lynch says the specific sentence communicates to the DM exactly the vibe to aim for.", "source": "Bryce Lynch, review of 'The Swan Hollow Intrigue', tenfootpole.org, 2026", "url": "https://tenfootpole.org/ironspike/?p=10575", "quote": "It communicates exactly the vibe the DM should be going for."}, {"index": 728, "feature": "adjective and adverb discipline", "claim": "Bryce Lynch says a single parenthetical adjective on a random-encounter entry tells the DM a great deal.", "source": "Bryce Lynch, review of 'The Swan Hollow Intrigue', tenfootpole.org, 2026", "url": "https://tenfootpole.org/ironspike/?p=10575", "quote": "That (dubious) in parens tells us a lot."}, {"index": 729, "feature": "adjective and adverb discipline", "claim": "Bryce Lynch says quick adjectives and adverbs describing an NPC work where a paragraph of italic read-aloud for the same NPC fails.", "source": "Bryce Lynch, review of 'The Swan Hollow Intrigue', tenfootpole.org, 2026", "url": "https://tenfootpole.org/ironspike/?p=10575", "quote": "quick adjectives/adverbs used to describe NPCs hit so well"}, {"index": 730, "feature": "plainness and economy", "claim": "Bryce Lynch's verdict on exposition in an adventure text is that more is not better.", "source": "Bryce Lynch, review of 'The Swan Hollow Intrigue', tenfootpole.org, 2026", "url": "https://tenfootpole.org/ironspike/?p=10575", "quote": "More is not better."}, {"index": 731, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "Justin Alexander rejects replacing borrowed material with generic versions, saying generic lacks identity.", "source": "Justin Alexander, 'Ptolus: Running the Campaign - From Table to Page', The Alexandrian, 2018", "url": "https://thealexandrian.net/wordpress/39322/roleplaying-games/ptolus-running-the-campaign-from-table-to-page", "quote": "“Generic” isn’t good. Generic lacks identity."}, {"index": 733, "feature": "civic record register", "claim": "Gus L says a game text made entirely of fruitful voids becomes at best a prose poem shaped like an instruction manual or gazetteer.", "source": "Gus L, 'Maxims of the OSR', All Dead Generations, 2023", "url": "https://alldeadgenerations.blogspot.com/2023/08/maxims-of-osr.html", "quote": "a mediocre at best prose poem in the form"}, {"index": 734, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "Bryce Lynch praises a keying pattern in which each room opens with a short vibe overview before its bulleted details.", "source": "Bryce Lynch, review of 'The Lions of Tell Arn Part 2', tenfootpole.org, 2026", "url": "https://tenfootpole.org/ironspike/?p=10550", "quote": "The rooms start with a kind of vibe overview."}, {"index": 735, "feature": "concrete sensory noun", "claim": "The room text Bryce Lynch holds up as the model is a string of verbless sensory noun phrases, quoted in his review.", "source": "module text by Nikoline quoted in Bryce Lynch's review, tenfootpole.org, 2026", "url": "https://tenfootpole.org/ironspike/?p=10550", "quote": "Sickly stench; grease and putrescence."}, {"index": 736, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "Bryce Lynch's test of a good room description is that it reads as if the writer had lived the room and got the mental image onto paper.", "source": "Bryce Lynch, review of 'The Lions of Tell Arn Part 2', tenfootpole.org, 2026", "url": "https://tenfootpole.org/ironspike/?p=10550", "quote": "someone called up a mental image in their head"}, {"index": 738, "feature": "edition and house style", "claim": "Bryce Lynch praises bolding and italics used sparingly to draw the eye rather than overused.", "source": "Bryce Lynch, review of 'The Lions of Tell Arn Part 2', tenfootpole.org, 2026", "url": "https://tenfootpole.org/ironspike/?p=10550", "quote": "Bolding and italics are used to effect to draw the eye"}, {"index": 739, "feature": "plainness and economy", "claim": "Bryce Lynch praises treasure descriptions that stay terse while being more interesting than a plus-one sword.", "source": "Bryce Lynch, review of 'The Lions of Tell Arn Part 2', tenfootpole.org, 2026", "url": "https://tenfootpole.org/ironspike/?p=10550", "quote": "terse but much more interesting than a Sword, +1"}, {"index": 740, "feature": "other: key format", "claim": "David Hartlage reports that Gygax's own Castle Greyhawk key usually gave each room a one-line note.", "source": "David Hartlage, 'How much description should a dungeon key include?', DMDavid, 2016", "url": "https://dmdavid.com/tag/how-much-description-should-a-dungeon-key-include/", "quote": "he usually wrote a 1-line note for each room"}, {"index": 741, "feature": "edition and house style", "claim": "David Hartlage says Gygax's generous location descriptions in G1 set the pattern for virtually every adventure that followed.", "source": "David Hartlage, 'How much description should a dungeon key include?', DMDavid, 2016", "url": "https://dmdavid.com/tag/how-much-description-should-a-dungeon-key-include/", "quote": "Gary’s longer descriptions set the pattern for virtually every adventure to follow."}, {"index": 742, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "David Hartlage argues longer descriptions spare the DM inventing details, whose first ideas tend toward the obvious.", "source": "David Hartlage, 'How much description should a dungeon key include?', DMDavid, 2016", "url": "https://dmdavid.com/tag/how-much-description-should-a-dungeon-key-include/", "quote": "the ideas that spring to mind first will steer toward the obvious"}, {"index": 744, "feature": "edition and house style", "claim": "David Hartlage says the custom of long descriptions makes authors write even when they have nothing to add.", "source": "David Hartlage, 'How much description should a dungeon key include?', DMDavid, 2016", "url": "https://dmdavid.com/tag/how-much-description-should-a-dungeon-key-include/", "quote": "encourages authors to write something even when they have little"}, {"index": 745, "feature": "omission as information", "claim": "David Hartlage says an avalanche of description buries the details that would actually reach play.", "source": "David Hartlage, 'How much description should a dungeon key include?', DMDavid, 2016", "url": "https://dmdavid.com/tag/how-much-description-should-a-dungeon-key-include/", "quote": "An avalanche of description does more than squander page count."}, {"index": 746, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "David Hartlage says a location's description should be as long as the way players will actually engage that location.", "source": "David Hartlage, 'How much description should a dungeon key include?', DMDavid, 2016", "url": "https://dmdavid.com/tag/how-much-description-should-a-dungeon-key-include/", "quote": "The length of descriptions should match the way players will engage"}, {"index": 747, "feature": "adjective and adverb discipline", "claim": "David Hartlage tells writers to keep the evocative details out from under lavish description of pallets and rubbish.", "source": "David Hartlage, 'How much description should a dungeon key include?', DMDavid, 2016", "url": "https://dmdavid.com/tag/how-much-description-should-a-dungeon-key-include/", "quote": "Don’t bury the evocative bits in lavish descriptions"}, {"index": 748, "feature": "concrete sensory noun", "claim": "David Hartlage says descriptions should carry telling details and information he can use in play.", "source": "David Hartlage, 'How much description should a dungeon key include?', DMDavid, 2016", "url": "https://dmdavid.com/tag/how-much-description-should-a-dungeon-key-include/", "quote": "Descriptions should focus on telling details and plot-critical information"}, {"index": 749, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "David Hartlage's test for cutting a description is whether it is the first thing a typical DM would imagine anyway.", "source": "David Hartlage, 'How much description should a dungeon key include?', DMDavid, 2016", "url": "https://dmdavid.com/tag/how-much-description-should-a-dungeon-key-include/", "quote": "the first thing a typical DM would imagine at the table"}, {"index": 750, "feature": "boxed text form", "claim": "David Hartlage grants that the boxed descriptions in Curse of Strahd are evocative while faulting their use in every empty room.", "source": "David Hartlage, 'How much description should a dungeon key include?', DMDavid, 2016", "url": "https://dmdavid.com/tag/how-much-description-should-a-dungeon-key-include/", "quote": "The descriptions are evocative"}, {"index": 751, "feature": "edition and house style", "claim": "David Hartlage quotes Bob Bledsaw in Dragon 27 saying judges regarded describing dungeons as their own domain.", "source": "Bob Bledsaw in Dragon #27, quoted by David Hartlage, DMDavid, 2016", "url": "https://dmdavid.com/tag/how-much-description-should-a-dungeon-key-include/", "quote": "judges felt that the actual description of dungeons was their ‘domain’"}, {"index": 752, "feature": "plainness and economy", "claim": "David Hartlage quotes Michael Curtis describing the Stonehell style as the minimum information needed to run the dungeon.", "source": "Michael Curtis, quoted by David Hartlage, DMDavid, 2016", "url": "https://dmdavid.com/tag/how-much-description-should-a-dungeon-key-include/", "quote": "the minimum amount of information needed to run the dungeon"}, {"index": 753, "feature": "other: key format", "claim": "Luke Gearing's rewrite of his own adventure consisted largely of turning its bullet points back into prose.", "source": "Luke Gearing, 'Bullet Points vs Prose', lukegearing.blot.im, 2023", "url": "https://lukegearing.blot.im/bullet-points-vs-prose", "quote": "A lot of this involves de-bullet pointing the text."}, {"index": 754, "feature": "other: key format", "claim": "Luke Gearing says his own bullet points were already more prose-based than most, which worsens rather than eases his objection to them.", "source": "Luke Gearing, 'Bullet Points vs Prose', lukegearing.blot.im, 2023", "url": "https://lukegearing.blot.im/bullet-points-vs-prose", "quote": "I find this only exacerbates my issue with them."}, {"index": 755, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "Luke Gearing's model prose keys a room in the present tense from a single dominant smell or object outward.", "source": "Luke Gearing's own demonstration passage, 'Bullet Points vs Prose', 2023", "url": "https://lukegearing.blot.im/bullet-points-vs-prose", "quote": "The smell of cold grease fills the air"}, {"index": 757, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "Luke Gearing's method for writing a place is to close his eyes and imagine standing in it.", "source": "Luke Gearing, 'Techniques to Write Adventures', lukegearing.blot.im, 2021", "url": "https://lukegearing.blot.im/techniques-to-write-adventures", "quote": "I often just close my eyes and imagine being there"}, {"index": 758, "feature": "concrete sensory noun", "claim": "Luke Gearing says that mental walk-through is for finding the touchstones that communicate a place's tone.", "source": "Luke Gearing, 'Techniques to Write Adventures', lukegearing.blot.im, 2021", "url": "https://lukegearing.blot.im/techniques-to-write-adventures", "quote": "finding those touchstones which communicate the tone"}, {"index": 760, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "Zach Howard judges the introduction to the Holmes Basic Sample Dungeon highly evocative, reading almost like weird fiction.", "source": "Zach Howard (Zenopus Archives), 'Part 46: \"Zenopus Built a Tower\"', 2015", "url": "https://zenopusarchives.blogspot.com/2015/02/part-46-zenopus-built-tower.html", "quote": "The introduction is highly evocative, reading almost like the weird fiction"}, {"index": 761, "feature": "boxed text form", "claim": "J. Eric Holmes instructs the Dungeon Master to read the sample dungeon's background material aloud to the assembled players.", "source": "J. Eric Holmes, D&D Basic Set (1977), quoted by Zach Howard (Zenopus Archives) 2015", "url": "https://zenopusarchives.blogspot.com/2015/02/part-46-zenopus-built-tower.html", "quote": "should read the background material above to the assembled players"}, {"index": 762, "feature": "boxed text form", "claim": "Howard reads Holmes's Portown background as material meant to be read to the players, predating boxed text.", "source": "Zach Howard (Zenopus Archives), 'Part 46', 2015", "url": "https://zenopusarchives.blogspot.com/2015/02/part-46-zenopus-built-tower.html", "quote": "this background material is meant to be read to the players"}, {"index": 763, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "Howard calls Holmes's one-paragraph account of Portown's trade, ships and inhabitants a thumbnail sketch of a campaign setting.", "source": "Zach Howard (Zenopus Archives), 'Part 46', 2015", "url": "https://zenopusarchives.blogspot.com/2015/02/part-46-zenopus-built-tower.html", "quote": "Here we get a thumbnail sketch of a campaign setting"}, {"index": 765, "feature": "terminology consistency", "claim": "TSR's published Basic Set changed Holmes's manuscript phrase 'one or more assistants' to 'one or more men-at-arms'.", "source": "Zach Howard (Zenopus Archives), 'Part 46', 2015", "url": "https://zenopusarchives.blogspot.com/2015/02/part-46-zenopus-built-tower.html", "quote": "changes \"one or more assistants\" to \"one or more men-at-arms\""}, {"index": 766, "feature": "dm-facing sentence", "claim": "TSR moved a sentence of Holmes's introduction to the end of its paragraph so it sat immediately before the room descriptions.", "source": "Zach Howard (Zenopus Archives), 'Part 46', 2015", "url": "https://zenopusarchives.blogspot.com/2015/02/part-46-zenopus-built-tower.html", "quote": "it places it right before the room descriptions"}, {"index": 767, "feature": "edition and house style", "claim": "Howard reports that TSR made only minor changes to Holmes's sample dungeon between manuscript and published rulebook.", "source": "Zach Howard (Zenopus Archives), 'Part 46', 2015", "url": "https://zenopusarchives.blogspot.com/2015/02/part-46-zenopus-built-tower.html", "quote": "only minor changes by TSR in the published version"}, {"index": 768, "feature": "boxed text form", "claim": "Delta dates the first boxed text in a Gygax adventure to the 1982 published Lost Caverns of Tsojcanth.", "source": "Delta (Daniel R. Collins), 'Early Evolution of Encounter Text', Delta's D&D Hotspot, 2021", "url": "https://deltasdnd.blogspot.com/2021/10/early-evolution-of-encounter-text.html", "quote": "And hey, there's boxed text for the first time!"}, {"index": 769, "feature": "boxed text form", "claim": "In the 1982 Lost Caverns of Tsojcanth the dungeon areas carry boxed text while the wilderness areas do not.", "source": "Delta (Daniel R. Collins), 'Early Evolution of Encounter Text', 2021", "url": "https://deltasdnd.blogspot.com/2021/10/early-evolution-of-encounter-text.html", "quote": "while the dungeon areas use boxed text, the wilderness does not"}, {"index": 770, "feature": "plainness and economy", "claim": "Gygax's 1974 Castle Greyhawk notes gave a single curt bullet-line per encounter area.", "source": "Delta (Daniel R. Collins), 'Early Evolution of Encounter Text', 2021", "url": "https://deltasdnd.blogspot.com/2021/10/early-evolution-of-encounter-text.html", "quote": "only a single curt bullet-line per encounter area"}, {"index": 772, "feature": "concrete sensory noun", "claim": "In the 1978 Steading of the Hill Giant Chief the area paragraphs tend to lead with sense-descriptions.", "source": "Delta (Daniel R. Collins), 'Early Evolution of Encounter Text', 2021", "url": "https://deltasdnd.blogspot.com/2021/10/early-evolution-of-encounter-text.html", "quote": "the paragraphs tend to lead with those sense-descriptions"}, {"index": 773, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "Expedition to the Barrier Peaks devotes almost 500 words to the Ship Commander's Quarters, an area with no monsters.", "source": "Delta (Daniel R. Collins), 'Early Evolution of Encounter Text', 2021", "url": "https://deltasdnd.blogspot.com/2021/10/early-evolution-of-encounter-text.html", "quote": "almost 500 words total for the one area"}, {"index": 774, "feature": "boxed text form", "claim": "Isle of the Ape opens with a boxed speech by Tenser of some 1,600 words to be read verbatim.", "source": "Delta (Daniel R. Collins), 'Early Evolution of Encounter Text', 2021", "url": "https://deltasdnd.blogspot.com/2021/10/early-evolution-of-encounter-text.html", "quote": "2 full pages of text to be read verbatim"}, {"index": 775, "feature": "boxed text form", "claim": "The Temple of Elemental Evil's introductory boxed text runs about 1,300 words, more than a full page.", "source": "Delta (Daniel R. Collins), 'Early Evolution of Encounter Text', 2021", "url": "https://deltasdnd.blogspot.com/2021/10/early-evolution-of-encounter-text.html", "quote": "introductory boxed text section that runs more than a full page"}, {"index": 776, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "Delta counts 1,200 words of content text for room 404 of the Temple of Elemental Evil, a 20 by 30 foot room.", "source": "Delta (Daniel R. Collins), 'Early Evolution of Encounter Text', 2021", "url": "https://deltasdnd.blogspot.com/2021/10/early-evolution-of-encounter-text.html", "quote": "room 404 is ironically a 20'×30' room with 1,200 words"}, {"index": 777, "feature": "dm-facing sentence", "claim": "The Temple of Elemental Evil tends to open an encounter with non-boxed information for the DM before the boxed description.", "source": "Delta (Daniel R. Collins), 'Early Evolution of Encounter Text', 2021", "url": "https://deltasdnd.blogspot.com/2021/10/early-evolution-of-encounter-text.html", "quote": "start with some non-boxed info to the DM, and then"}, {"index": 779, "feature": "boxed text form", "claim": "Delta says boxing his own sensory lead-in would imply reading it verbatim, which he calls a bad idea for a volatile situation.", "source": "Delta (Daniel R. Collins), comment reply on 'Early Evolution of Encounter Text', 2021", "url": "https://deltasdnd.blogspot.com/2021/10/early-evolution-of-encounter-text.html", "quote": "that seems to imply the need to read it verbatim"}, {"index": 780, "feature": "boxed text form", "claim": "A reader argues boxed text is linear and assumes the characters approach a room from one specific direction.", "source": "Warren D., reader comment on Delta's D&D Hotspot, 2021", "url": "https://deltasdnd.blogspot.com/2021/10/early-evolution-of-encounter-text.html", "quote": "because it is linear, it often assumes PCs are approaching the room"}, {"index": 782, "feature": "boxed text form", "claim": "In the 1979 Lost Tamoachan the initial area descriptions are set as quoted and indented passages rather than boxed text.", "source": "Delta (Daniel R. Collins), 'Tip-Offs in Tamoachan', Delta's D&D Hotspot, 2017", "url": "https://deltasdnd.blogspot.com/2017/10/tip-offs-in-tamoachan.html", "quote": "not exactly in boxed text, but in specially quoted and indented"}, {"index": 783, "feature": "edition and house style", "claim": "Bruce Cordell wrote that the 4e tactical format makes a compelling adventure narrative difficult to generate.", "source": "Bruce Cordell, Design & Development column (2011), quoted by Mike Bridges, Greyhawkery", "url": "https://greyhawkery.blogspot.com/2011/06/new-article-d-adventure-format.html", "quote": "The tactical format makes it difficult to generate a compelling adventure narrative"}, {"index": 784, "feature": "omission as information", "claim": "Cordell wrote that the tactical format leaves too little space for notes on exploration, lore or character story.", "source": "Bruce Cordell, Design & Development column (2011), quoted by Mike Bridges, Greyhawkery", "url": "https://greyhawkery.blogspot.com/2011/06/new-article-d-adventure-format.html", "quote": "adventures tend to be fairly Spartan affairs, because too little space"}, {"index": 785, "feature": "edition and house style", "claim": "Cordell reports reviewing 1st Edition adventures including Tomb of Horrors for models of adventure format.", "source": "Bruce Cordell, Design & Development column (2011), quoted by Mike Bridges, Greyhawkery", "url": "https://greyhawkery.blogspot.com/2011/06/new-article-d-adventure-format.html", "quote": "1st Edition adventures I looked at included Tomb of Horrors"}, {"index": 787, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "In 4th Edition adventures, locations with no encounter are compressed into single paragraphs with minimal description.", "source": "Welcome to the Deathtrap (OSR blog), 'Designing Layout and Information in Adventures', 2021", "url": "https://deathtrap-games.blogspot.com/2021/08/designing-layout-and-information-in.html", "quote": "compressed into single paragraphs with minimal effort spent on description"}, {"index": 788, "feature": "boxed text form", "claim": "The blogger describes 5th Edition's Candlekeep Mysteries as a wall of text with a boxed description.", "source": "Welcome to the Deathtrap (OSR blog), 2021", "url": "https://deathtrap-games.blogspot.com/2021/08/designing-layout-and-information-in.html", "quote": "it is a wall of text with a boxed description"}, {"index": 791, "feature": "boxed text form", "claim": "The Dark of Hot Springs Island replaces long box text with a handful of adjectives for the GM to improvise from.", "source": "Welcome to the Deathtrap (OSR blog), 2021", "url": "https://deathtrap-games.blogspot.com/2021/08/designing-layout-and-information-in.html", "quote": "rather than give a long box text, Hot Springs Island provides"}, {"index": 792, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "Appelcline calls the Village of Hommlet Greyhawk's first described locale that was not just a dungeon or adventuring point.", "source": "Shannon Appelcline, 'Designers & Dragons: The Column #24', RPGnet, 2013", "url": "https://www.rpg.net/columns/designers-and-dragons/designers-and-dragons24.phtml", "quote": "first described locale that wasn't just some dungeon or adventuring point"}, {"index": 793, "feature": "edition and house style", "claim": "Appelcline says the TSR UK Saltmarsh adventures differ markedly from the staid dungeon crawls US authors were then writing.", "source": "Shannon Appelcline, 'Designers & Dragons: The Column #24', RPGnet, 2013", "url": "https://www.rpg.net/columns/designers-and-dragons/designers-and-dragons24.phtml", "quote": "how much they differ from the staid dungeon crawls then being"}, {"index": 797, "feature": "edition and house style", "claim": "Merwin judges the way The Village of Hommlet hands over its hook and opening not very exciting or particularly nutritious.", "source": "Shawn Merwin, 'Let's Design an Adventure: Adventure Hooks and Opening Scenes', D&D Beyond, 2019", "url": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/posts/624-lets-design-an-adventure-adventure-hooks-and", "quote": "It’s not very exciting or particularly nutritious"}, {"index": 798, "feature": "boxed text form", "claim": "Merwin says an encounter's opening information is probably delivered in the form of boxed text.", "source": "Shawn Merwin, 'Let's Design an Adventure: Encounter Basics', D&D Beyond, 2019", "url": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/posts/626-lets-design-an-adventure-encounter-basics", "quote": "probably in the form of boxed text or some other vehicle"}, {"index": 799, "feature": "withheld information and inference", "claim": "Merwin locates drama in the dissonance between what characters know entering an encounter and what they learn inside it.", "source": "Shawn Merwin, 'Let's Design an Adventure: Encounter Basics', D&D Beyond, 2019", "url": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/posts/626-lets-design-an-adventure-encounter-basics", "quote": "There is drama and tension in the dissonance between the information"}, {"index": 800, "feature": "dm-facing sentence", "claim": "Peterson says In Search of the Unknown carried starting guidance on running an adventure absent from the then-current D&D books.", "source": "Jon Peterson, Game Wizards (MIT Press 2021), excerpt published by Polygon", "url": "https://www.polygon.com/2021/10/12/22722602/dungeons-dragons-game-wizards-book-excerpt-jon-peterson-arneson-lawsuit", "quote": "useful starting guidance on running an adventure that you wouldn’t find"}, {"index": 801, "feature": "edition and house style", "claim": "Peterson says much of Carr's text on the art of dungeon mastering was effectively paraphrased in Gygax's Keep on the Borderlands.", "source": "Jon Peterson, Game Wizards (MIT Press 2021), excerpt published by Polygon", "url": "https://www.polygon.com/2021/10/12/22722602/dungeons-dragons-game-wizards-book-excerpt-jon-peterson-arneson-lawsuit", "quote": "enlightening text about the art of dungeon mastering was effectively paraphrased"}, {"index": 802, "feature": "edition and house style", "claim": "Peterson dates the start of modules as a business for TSR to the summer of 1978.", "source": "Jon Peterson, Game Wizards (MIT Press 2021), excerpt published by Polygon", "url": "https://www.polygon.com/2021/10/12/22722602/dungeons-dragons-game-wizards-book-excerpt-jon-peterson-arneson-lawsuit", "quote": "Modules only started to become a business for TSR in the summer"}, {"index": 804, "feature": "terminology consistency", "claim": "Peterson notes Arneson still wrote armour class values in Roman numerals in his 1977 tournament documents.", "source": "Jon Peterson, 'Dave Arneson's Origins 1977 Tournament', Playing at the World, 2020", "url": "https://playingattheworld.blogspot.com/2020/04/dave-arnesons-origins-1977-tournament.html", "quote": "Arneson still wrote armor class values with Roman numerals"}, {"index": 806, "feature": "plainness and economy", "claim": "The same writeup says the Gazetteers make their points quickly and efficiently.", "source": "Rulebook Heavily, 'Dungeons & Dragons Gazetteer' writeup, RPG Writeups", "url": "https://writeups.letsyouandhimfight.com/rulebook-heavily/dungeonsanddragons-gazetteer/", "quote": "points are made quickly and efficiently"}, {"index": 807, "feature": "plainness and economy", "claim": "GAZ1 The Grand Duchy of Karameikos runs to 64 pages plus maps.", "source": "Rulebook Heavily, 'Dungeons & Dragons Gazetteer' writeup, RPG Writeups", "url": "https://writeups.letsyouandhimfight.com/rulebook-heavily/dungeonsanddragons-gazetteer/", "quote": "The first one clocks in at a mere 64 pages plus maps"}, {"index": 809, "feature": "civic record register", "claim": "GAZ1 gives Specularum an official census of 50.000 taken fifteen years before the setting's present.", "source": "Rulebook Heavily, 'Dungeons & Dragons Gazetteer' writeup, RPG Writeups", "url": "https://writeups.letsyouandhimfight.com/rulebook-heavily/dungeonsanddragons-gazetteer/", "quote": "the official census of 50.000 was taken 15 years ago"}, {"index": 810, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "In GAZ1 some notable places of Karameikos merit only a paragraph of description.", "source": "Rulebook Heavily, 'Dungeons & Dragons Gazetteer' writeup, RPG Writeups", "url": "https://writeups.letsyouandhimfight.com/rulebook-heavily/dungeonsanddragons-gazetteer/", "quote": "Some merit only a paragraph"}, {"index": 811, "feature": "plainness and economy", "claim": "An essayist characterises J. Eric Holmes's Basic Set prose as dispassionate, methodical and encouraging.", "source": "Serban Ionita, 'The Shadowed Old Gods of Game Design', The RPG Gazette, 2025", "url": "https://therpggazette.wordpress.com/2025/11/11/the-shadowed-old-gods-of-game-design-remembering-holmes-moldvay-and-mentzer/", "quote": "His prose is dispassionate, methodical, and encouraging"}, {"index": 812, "feature": "edition and house style", "claim": "The same essay calls the original 1974 boxed set's text dense and peculiar.", "source": "Serban Ionita, 'The Shadowed Old Gods of Game Design', The RPG Gazette, 2025", "url": "https://therpggazette.wordpress.com/2025/11/11/the-shadowed-old-gods-of-game-design-remembering-holmes-moldvay-and-mentzer/", "quote": "The text in the set was dense and peculiar"}, {"index": 813, "feature": "plainness and economy", "claim": "The same essay calls Tom Moldvay's 1981 Basic text both efficient and lyrical.", "source": "Serban Ionita, 'The Shadowed Old Gods of Game Design', The RPG Gazette, 2025", "url": "https://therpggazette.wordpress.com/2025/11/11/the-shadowed-old-gods-of-game-design-remembering-holmes-moldvay-and-mentzer/", "quote": "Moldvay’s text seems both efficient and lyrical"}, {"index": 815, "feature": "omission as information", "claim": "The essay says Moldvay's adventures implied a cosmology, a history and a mood through their placement of statues, monsters and ruins.", "source": "Serban Ionita, 'The Shadowed Old Gods of Game Design', The RPG Gazette, 2025", "url": "https://therpggazette.wordpress.com/2025/11/11/the-shadowed-old-gods-of-game-design-remembering-holmes-moldvay-and-mentzer/", "quote": "Each adventure implied a cosmology, a history, and a mood"}, {"index": 816, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "The essay says Moldvay's adventures communicated worldviews rather than merely offering maps.", "source": "Serban Ionita, 'The Shadowed Old Gods of Game Design', The RPG Gazette, 2025", "url": "https://therpggazette.wordpress.com/2025/11/11/the-shadowed-old-gods-of-game-design-remembering-holmes-moldvay-and-mentzer/", "quote": "These adventures communicated worldviews, rather than merely offering maps"}, {"index": 819, "feature": "withheld information and inference", "claim": "A D&D Beyond article credits Carl Sargent with implying terrifying things through a few carefully chosen words in Iuz the Evil.", "source": "John Roy, 'Greyhawk Returns in the 2024 Dungeon Master's Guide', D&D Beyond, 2024", "url": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/posts/1834-greyhawk-returns-in-the-2024-dungeon-masters-guide", "quote": "implying terrifying things with a few carefully chosen words"}, {"index": 820, "feature": "civic record register", "claim": "The article says Gygax's settlement populations and locations followed real-world economic maxims.", "source": "John Roy, 'Greyhawk Returns in the 2024 Dungeon Master's Guide', D&D Beyond, 2024", "url": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/posts/1834-greyhawk-returns-in-the-2024-dungeon-masters-guide", "quote": "His settlement populations and locations followed real-world economic maxims"}, {"index": 821, "feature": "civic record register", "claim": "The article says Gygax gave each nation its imports, exports and the trade routes used to carry them.", "source": "John Roy, 'Greyhawk Returns in the 2024 Dungeon Master's Guide', D&D Beyond, 2024", "url": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/posts/1834-greyhawk-returns-in-the-2024-dungeon-masters-guide", "quote": "each nation's imports and exports and the trade routes they used"}, {"index": 822, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "The 2024 Dungeon Master's Guide gazetteer highlights dozens of locations across the Flanaess.", "source": "John Roy, 'Greyhawk Returns in the 2024 Dungeon Master's Guide', D&D Beyond, 2024", "url": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/posts/1834-greyhawk-returns-in-the-2024-dungeon-masters-guide", "quote": "highlights dozens of locations across the Flanaess"}, {"index": 823, "feature": "dm-facing sentence", "claim": "The article tells DMs to emphasise in their descriptions that Oerth is a world much like our own late Middle Ages.", "source": "John Roy, 'Greyhawk Returns in the 2024 Dungeon Master's Guide', D&D Beyond, 2024", "url": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/posts/1834-greyhawk-returns-in-the-2024-dungeon-masters-guide", "quote": "emphasize that this is a world much like our own"}, {"index": 824, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "A reviewer says the 2024 DMG's Greyhawk material serves as a prime example of what a setting can look like.", "source": "brimmels, 'In-Depth Review of the 2024 Dungeon Master's Guide', EN World, 2024", "url": "https://www.enworld.org/threads/in-depth-review-of-the-2024-dungeon-masters-guide.707861/", "quote": "it's a prime example of what a setting can look like"}, {"index": 825, "feature": "plainness and economy", "claim": "Zeb Cook says TSR's early module settings were held to no more than what would fit on a page.", "source": "David 'Zeb' Cook, Kobold Press world building seminar transcript, part 1, 2025", "url": "https://koboldpress.com/world-building-design-seminar-with-wolfgang-baur-zeb-cook-and-richard-green-part-1/", "quote": "We made no more than what we could put on a page"}, {"index": 826, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "Cook says every early TSR module required a quick setting created only so the module could be explained.", "source": "David 'Zeb' Cook, Kobold Press world building seminar transcript, part 1, 2025", "url": "https://koboldpress.com/world-building-design-seminar-with-wolfgang-baur-zeb-cook-and-richard-green-part-1/", "quote": "For every module, you had to create a quick setting"}, {"index": 827, "feature": "omission as information", "claim": "Cook reports readers wrote in convinced TSR held much more setting material than the page contained.", "source": "David 'Zeb' Cook, Kobold Press world building seminar transcript, part 1, 2025", "url": "https://koboldpress.com/world-building-design-seminar-with-wolfgang-baur-zeb-cook-and-richard-green-part-1/", "quote": "You obviously have a whole bunch more"}, {"index": 829, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "Baur calls the Forgotten Realms the kitchen sink approach to world building.", "source": "Wolfgang Baur, Kobold Press world building seminar transcript, part 1, 2025", "url": "https://koboldpress.com/world-building-design-seminar-with-wolfgang-baur-zeb-cook-and-richard-green-part-1/", "quote": "The Forgotten Realms is the kitchen sink approach to world building"}, {"index": 830, "feature": "plainness and economy", "claim": "Baur describes a newer one-book model as providing the minimum viable world building.", "source": "Wolfgang Baur, Kobold Press world building seminar transcript, part 1, 2025", "url": "https://koboldpress.com/world-building-design-seminar-with-wolfgang-baur-zeb-cook-and-richard-green-part-1/", "quote": "to provide the minimum viable world building"}, {"index": 831, "feature": "place and institution description", "claim": "Cook says the worst world building books he has read are literally just lists of places and people.", "source": "David 'Zeb' Cook, Kobold Press world building seminar transcript, part 2, 2025", "url": "https://koboldpress.com/world-building-design-seminar-with-wolfgang-baur-zeb-cook-and-richard-green-part-2/", "quote": "are ones that are literally just lists of places and people"}, {"index": 835, "feature": "withheld information and inference", "claim": "Baur says the expected arc of play should be stated explicitly to players rather than kept coy.", "source": "Wolfgang Baur, Kobold Press world building seminar transcript, part 2, 2025", "url": "https://koboldpress.com/world-building-design-seminar-with-wolfgang-baur-zeb-cook-and-richard-green-part-2/", "quote": "you should be explicit about it. Don’t be coy"}, {"index": 837, "feature": "omission as information", "claim": "The reviewer reads the Kobold Guide as telling setting designers they need not create all the details necessary for play.", "source": "Gnome Stew review of the Kobold Guide to Worldbuilding", "url": "https://gnomestew.com/review-the-kobold-guide-to-plots-and-campaigns-and-the-kobold-guide-to-worldbuilding/", "quote": "we don’t have to create all of the details necessary"}, {"index": 838, "feature": "omission as information", "claim": "The reviewer says the book advises leaving the finer mechanics to the GMs and players who pick up the work.", "source": "Gnome Stew review of the Kobold Guide to Worldbuilding", "url": "https://gnomestew.com/review-the-kobold-guide-to-plots-and-campaigns-and-the-kobold-guide-to-worldbuilding/", "quote": "it is better to leave the finer mechanics to the GMs and players"}, {"index": 841, "feature": "point of view and distance", "claim": "Mizer holds that a cosmographer is not so much a worldbuilder as a world-hopper.", "source": "Nicholas J. Mizer, 'The Role of the Cosmographer', International Journal of Role-playing 18, 2025", "url": "https://journals.uu.se/IJRP/article/download/1197/1052/4802", "quote": "so much a worldbuilder as a world-hopper"}, {"index": 851, "feature": "boxed text form", "claim": "Noonan reports that a DM reading boxed text holds player attention for two sentences and no further.", "source": "Jesse Decker and David Noonan, 'Design & Development: Undercover at Gen Con, Part 1', Wizards of the Coast, 2005", "url": "http://www.wizards.com/default.asp?x=dnd/dd/20050916a", "quote": "If you’re the DM, you get two sentences. Period."}, {"index": 852, "feature": "boxed text form", "claim": "Noonan's hypothesis was that boxed text longer than a paragraph probably is not worth reading aloud.", "source": "Jesse Decker and David Noonan, 'Design & Development: Undercover at Gen Con, Part 1', Wizards of the Coast, 2005", "url": "http://www.wizards.com/default.asp?x=dnd/dd/20050916a", "quote": "boxed text longer than a paragraph probably isn’t worth reading"}, {"index": 853, "feature": "boxed text form", "claim": "Noonan observed players repeatedly missing the actual data inside the boxed text, such as room dimensions and exits.", "source": "Jesse Decker and David Noonan, 'Design & Development: Undercover at Gen Con, Part 1', Wizards of the Coast, 2005", "url": "http://www.wizards.com/default.asp?x=dnd/dd/20050916a", "quote": "players were missing the actual data in the boxed text"}, {"index": 856, "feature": "cadence and rhythm", "claim": "Noonan concludes that oration is inherently harder to concentrate on than conversation.", "source": "Jesse Decker and David Noonan, 'Design & Development: Undercover at Gen Con, Part 1', Wizards of the Coast, 2005", "url": "http://www.wizards.com/default.asp?x=dnd/dd/20050916a", "quote": "oration is inherently harder to concentrate on than conversation"}, {"index": 857, "feature": "dm-facing sentence", "claim": "Noonan notes DMs are by and large reading the boxed text cold, from a script never uttered aloud before.", "source": "Jesse Decker and David Noonan, 'Design & Development: Undercover at Gen Con, Part 1', Wizards of the Coast, 2005", "url": "http://www.wizards.com/default.asp?x=dnd/dd/20050916a", "quote": "DMs are, by and large, reading the boxed text cold"}, {"index": 858, "feature": "edition and house style", "claim": "Writing in 2005, the authors say boxed text in adventures used to be a lot longer than it was then.", "source": "Jesse Decker and David Noonan, 'Design & Development: Undercover at Gen Con, Part 1', Wizards of the Coast, 2005", "url": "http://www.wizards.com/default.asp?x=dnd/dd/20050916a", "quote": "used to be a lot longer than it is today"}, {"index": 859, "feature": "boxed text form", "claim": "The authors advise the DM to ditch boxed text and use his own words for the initial description of the room.", "source": "Jesse Decker and David Noonan, 'Design & Development: Undercover at Gen Con, Part 1', Wizards of the Coast, 2005", "url": "http://www.wizards.com/default.asp?x=dnd/dd/20050916a", "quote": "use your own words for the initial description of the room"}, {"index": 861, "feature": "boxed text form", "claim": "Dyson Logos dates boxed and read-aloud text to the 1e period of published adventures.", "source": "Dyson Logos, 'The Dreaded Boxed Text', Dyson's Dodecahedron, 2013", "url": "https://dysonlogos.blog/2013/09/29/the-dreaded-boxed-text/", "quote": "During the 1e days, we saw the advent of “boxed” or “read-aloud” text"}, {"index": 862, "feature": "boxed text form", "claim": "Dyson attributes boxed text to tournament use, whose goal was to standardize the experience for each play group.", "source": "Dyson Logos, 'The Dreaded Boxed Text', Dyson's Dodecahedron, 2013", "url": "https://dysonlogos.blog/2013/09/29/the-dreaded-boxed-text/", "quote": "the goal being to standardize the game experience for each play group"}, {"index": 863, "feature": "point of view and distance", "claim": "Dyson faults boxed text for predicting the future for the party instead of leaving that to the players.", "source": "Dyson Logos, 'The Dreaded Boxed Text', Dyson's Dodecahedron, 2013", "url": "https://dysonlogos.blog/2013/09/29/the-dreaded-boxed-text/", "quote": "it predicts the future for the party"}, {"index": 864, "feature": "boxed text form", "claim": "Dyson urges writers to replace boxed text with a point-form list of things seen in the room.", "source": "Dyson Logos, 'The Dreaded Boxed Text', Dyson's Dodecahedron, 2013", "url": "https://dysonlogos.blog/2013/09/29/the-dreaded-boxed-text/", "quote": "replace it with a point-form list of things seen in the room"}, {"index": 866, "feature": "omission as information", "claim": "Jara argues setting and rules frame the game because they invariably imply reductions of the imagined world.", "source": "David Jara, 'Framings and Paratexts in Tabletop Role-playing Games', International Journal of Role-Playing 4, 2013", "url": "https://journals.uu.se/IJRP/article/download/228/252", "quote": "reductions of the imagined world"}, {"index": 867, "feature": "civic record register", "claim": "Jara treats the information a game setting gives as otherwise purely factual, to which intra-textual framing devices add diegetic meaning.", "source": "David Jara, 'Framings and Paratexts in Tabletop Role-playing Games', International Journal of Role-Playing 4, 2013", "url": "https://journals.uu.se/IJRP/article/download/228/252", "quote": "factual information given by the game setting"}], "verdicts": [{"index": 725, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 726, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 727, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 728, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 729, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 730, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 731, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 733, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 734, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 735, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 736, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 738, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 739, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 740, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 741, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 742, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 744, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 745, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 746, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 747, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 748, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 749, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 750, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 751, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 752, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 753, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 754, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 755, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 757, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round"}, {"index": 758, "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE", "trueWording": "", "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent s